      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "If the env var is not set and no config is cached, the flag falls back to `False` with no warning. Every public method then becomes an event handler again. This happens for any entry point other than `reflex run`, for example `uvicorn`/`gunicorn` pointed at the app module, or a test suite that imports the state modules.\n\nRepro with an app that sets `state_explicit_event_handlers=True`, and no `REFLEX_STATE_EXPLICIT_EVENT_HANDLERS` in the environment:\n\n```python\nfrom uvicorn.importer import import_from_string\nimport_from_string(\"pr7033_repro.pr7033_repro:app\")\nsorted(ExplicitState.event_handlers)\n# [..., 'mixin_helper', ..., 'step', ...]  <- undecorated helpers are handlers\n```\n\nThe docs mention this. But for a security switch, a silent fallback to the permissive mode is the wrong failure. Please make it fail closed. For example: record the value the first user State was built with. When the config is loaded (e.g. in `App.__init__`), raise if the config value differs.\n",
        "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/config.py",
        "line": 988
      },
      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "The exported env var also wins over later `rxconfig.py` edits, because `update_from_env` applies `REFLEX_*` over the file values. In dev, the worker reloads with the value from startup.\n\nRepro: start `reflex run` with `state_explicit_event_handlers=False`, then set it to `True` and save. After the reload, `step` and `mixin_helper` are still registered handlers. Only a full restart applies the change. The reverse edit (`True` to `False`) is also ignored.\n\nPlease do not let the persisted value override the file, or document that a change to this flag needs a restart.\n",
        "path": "reflex/utils/exec.py",
        "line": 581
      },
      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "This check runs only when a trigger is compiled. The runtime paths through `Event.from_event_type` and `fix_events` do not call it. Both cases below work in implicit mode, so users who migrate will hit them:\n\n- `app.add_page(page, on_load=State.undecorated)` is accepted. Every load of the page then fails in the backend with `ValueError: Unexpected event type, <class 'function'>`. The browser shows no error, and `on_load` does not run.\n- An event handler that does `return State.undecorated` (or yields it) fails with `TypeError: Your handler State.chain must only return/yield: None, Events...`.\n\nNeither message mentions `@rx.event` or `state_explicit_event_handlers`. Please call this check on the runtime path too, so that `on_load` and return/yield values get the same targeted error. Please also add tests for both cases.\n",
        "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/event/__init__.py",
        "line": 2368
      },
      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "A decorator without `functools.wraps` on top of `@rx.event` loses this marker. So the method silently stops being an event handler:\n\n```python\ndef require_login(f):\n    def inner(self, *a, **k):\n        return f(self, *a, **k)\n    return inner\n\nclass S(rx.State):\n    @require_login\n    @rx.event\n    def save(self): ...\n\nrx.button(on_click=S.save)\n```\n```\nEventFnArgMismatchError: Event on_click only provides 1 arguments, but <function require_login.<locals>.inner ...> requires at least 3 arguments to be passed to the event handler.\n```\n\nThe wrapper's `__qualname__` is `require_login.<locals>.inner`, so `_mark_undecorated_methods` also skips it, and the targeted error does not fire. The same code works on `main`. It also works with `functools.wraps`, or with `@rx.event` as the outer decorator.\n\nPlease give this case a clear error that names the flag. Or at least document that `@rx.event` must be the outermost decorator, or that wrappers must use `functools.wraps`.\n",
        "path": "reflex/state.py",
        "line": 848
      },
      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "This `setattr` runs in the default mode too. So `rx.event(x)` now raises for any `x` without a `__dict__`:\n\n```python\nrx.event(Obj().m)\n# AttributeError: 'method' object has no attribute '_rx_event' and no __dict__ for setting new attributes\nrx.event(len)\n# AttributeError: 'builtin_function_or_method' object has no attribute '_rx_event' ...\n```\n\nOn `main`, both calls return the value unchanged. I did not find this usage in the repo or the docs, but it is a behavior change for users who do not enable the flag. Please guard the `setattr`, or give a clear error.\n",
        "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/event/__init__.py",
        "line": 3188
      },
      {
        "by": "FarhanAliRaza",
        "outdated": false,
        "first_comment": "These two markers are internal, but adding them to `EventNamespace` makes them public as `rx.event.EVENT_MARKER` and `rx.event.UNDECORATED_STATE_METHOD_MARKER`. `reflex/state.py` imports them from `reflex_base.event` directly, so the namespace entries are not needed. Please remove them, so that no new public API has to be supported.\n",
        "path": "packages/reflex-base/src/reflex_base/event/__init__.py",
        "line": 3060
      }
    ]
  },
